     "><!--[0--><!--[0--><i class="sp-icn inline-block align-middle i-sp-28 !w-[62px] !h-[62px] scale-[40%] -m-5 -mr-4 md:scale-[45%]  flex-shrink-0 !scale-[34%] -ml-[1.3rem] -mr-[1.3rem]"></i><!--]--><!--]--> <b class="truncate">All Sports</b><!----></button><!--]--><!----> <button class=" relative flex flex-none items-center gap-1.5 bg-[#f2fbff] font-bold rounded-r-full border border-olbg-blue-med-2 border-l-0 hover:text-olbg-link-hover py-2 px-3 md:px-5 md:py-1  "><!--[-1--><!--]--> <span class="w-3 h-4 flex flex-col gap-[2px] justify-center before:content after:content before:h-[2px] after:h-[2px] before:bg-current after:bg-current before:rounded-full after:rounded-full scale-[99%]
            md:-translate-x-0.5
            false
         "><span class="h-[2px] bg-current rounded-full w-1/2 order-last"></span></span><!----> <span class="text-[10px]"><span class="max-md:hidden">SPORTS</span> MENU</span></button></div><!----></div><!--]--> <!--[-1--><!--]--> <!--[-1--><!--]--></div> <div data-modal-id="tips-custom" class="fixed mdl-center opacity-0 pointer-events-none z-modal fixed inset-0 flex justify-center items-start "><div style="min-height:unset" class=" animate-none modal false z-modal shadow animate-duration-[200ms] bg-white animate-duration-[150ms] max-md:fixed max-md:inset-0 false md:relative md:w-[90vw] lg:w-[55vw] md:rounded-lg md:overflow-hidden md:border-c-border-2 md:top-10 false  md:!w-[50vw] lg:!w-[40vw] "><!--[-1--><!--]--> <!--[0--><button type="button" aria-label="Close dialog" class="absolute flex items-center justify-center top-0 right-0 w-[2.5rem] h-[2.5rem] text-olbg-dark-blue hover:text-olbg-link-hover pt-4"><i class="text-xs i-ui-cross"></i></button><!--]--></div></div><!----><!----><!----></div> <!--[-1--><!--]--> <!----><!----> <!----><!----> <!--[-1--><!--]--></div> <!--[-1--><!--]--> <!--[0--><div class="container"><p class="th-red p-2 rounded flex flex-col flex-wrap gap-1 my-3 md:flex-row md:items-center"><span class="relative max-md:pl-7 md:flex md:gap-2 md:items-center"><span>No tips found.</span></span></p><!----></div><!--]--> <section><div class="container"><!--[--><p class="!my-3 th-info p-2 rounded flex gap-2 items-baseline text-pretty md:pr-5 "><i class="i-ui-info text-lg translate-y-px"></i> <span><!----><b>Experts</b> are OLBG tipsters with high strike rates, current month or 6 month profitability for a particular sport.<!----></span></p><p class="!my-3 th-info p-2 rounded flex gap-2 items-baseline text-pretty md:pr-5 "><i class="i-ui-info text-lg translate-y-px"></i> <span><!----><b>Value Rating</b> is based on comparing the odds of a selection to the theoretical probability of winning based on the percentage of tips for it.<!----></span></p><!--]--><!----></div></section> <!--[0--><div class="article"><main class="article-content"><!--[--><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 article-intro pt-3"><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="how-our-greyhound-racing-tips-work">How Our Greyhound Racing Tips Work</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>Each day, this page shows the most popular free greyhound racing tips and predictions from OLBG members for today's UK meetings.</p><p>Every race listed above shows the most-tipped dog, its current odds, how many win tips it has received and its share of the tips in that race. The star value rating compares the dog's odds with its chance of winning as implied by that share of the tips, so the more stars, the more generous the price looks against the weight of tipping support.</p><p>To back a selection, use the Add button to put it straight into your betslip, or click through to the race page for the full picture on every runner.</p><p>Greyhound racing runs from late morning to late evening, with races only minutes apart, so it's easy to bet more often than you intended. Set a budget before you start, stick to races you've studied, and never chase losses. Betting is for over-18s only. If gambling stops being fun, free and confidential support is available from <a href="https://www.gamcare.org.uk/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GamCare</a> and <a href="https://www.gambleaware.org/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">GambleAware</a>.</p><p>New to betting on the dogs? Our <a href="/bookmakers/articles/greyhounds-betting-advice">greyhound betting advice</a> guide covers the basics.</p><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="how-to-find-today-s-greyhound-tips-race-by-race">How to Find Today's Greyhound Tips Race by Race</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>Click on any race above to open its race page. There you'll see two headline picks: the ‘Most Popular Tip’, which is the dog with the most tips from OLBG members, and the ‘Best Tipsters Tip’, which is the pick backed by the tipsters with the strongest greyhound records.</p><p>Below those, every runner is listed with its tips and the comments behind them. Each tip shows the tipster's greyhound profit and strike rate, so you can judge how much weight to give each prediction.</p><p>If you'd rather follow individual tipsters than individual races, see the full rankings on our <a href="/best-tipsters/Greyhounds/28">best greyhound tipsters</a> page.</p><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="which-uk-greyhound-tracks-do-our-tips-cover">Which UK Greyhound Tracks Do Our Tips Cover?</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>Our greyhound tips cover racing at tracks licensed by the <a href="https://www.gbgb.org.uk/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Greyhound Board of Great Britain</a> (GBGB), listed below with the main race distances run at each.</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Track</th><th scope="col">Sprint</th><th scope="col">Standard</th><th scope="col">Standard (second trip)</th><th scope="col">Staying</th><th scope="col">Marathon</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Central Park</strong></td><td>265m</td><td>450m</td><td>480m &amp; 500m</td><td>642m &amp; 708m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Doncaster</strong></td><td>275m</td><td>450m</td><td>483m</td><td>661m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dunstall Park</strong></td><td>270m</td><td>480m</td><td><br></td><td>660m &amp; 715m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Harlow</strong></td><td>238m</td><td>415m</td><td>592m</td><td>769m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hove</strong></td><td>285m</td><td>500m</td><td>515m</td><td>695m &amp; 740m</td><td>955m</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Kinsley</strong></td><td>268m</td><td>462m</td><td><br></td><td>650m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Monmore</strong></td><td>264m</td><td>480m</td><td><br></td><td>630m &amp; 684m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Newcastle</strong></td><td>290m</td><td>480m</td><td><br></td><td>640m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nottingham</strong></td><td>255m &amp; 305m</td><td>480m</td><td>500m</td><td>680m &amp; 730m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pelaw Grange</strong></td><td><br></td><td>435m</td><td>590m</td><td><br></td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Romford</strong></td><td>225m</td><td>400m</td><td>575m</td><td>750m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sheffield</strong></td><td>280m</td><td>480m</td><td>500m</td><td>660m &amp; 720m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sunderland</strong></td><td>261m</td><td>450m</td><td><br></td><td>640m</td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Towcester</strong></td><td>270m</td><td>500m</td><td><br></td><td>712m</td><td>942m</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Valley</strong></td><td>260m</td><td>460m</td><td><br></td><td><br></td><td><br></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Yarmouth</strong></td><td>277m</td><td>462m</td><td><br></td><td>659m</td><td><br></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>How greyhound races are graded</h3><p>Graded races make up most of the daily cards. Each race carries a letter for its distance category and a number for its class, with 1 the highest grade (so A1 is stronger than A5). The most common prefixes you'll see on a racecard are:</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Prefix</th><th scope="col">Race type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td>Standard-distance graded race</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td>Sprint</td></tr><tr><td><strong>S</strong></td><td>Stayers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>E</strong></td><td>Marathon</td></tr><tr><td><strong>P</strong></td><td>Puppy race</td></tr><tr><td><strong>OR</strong></td><td>Open race, where dogs are selected on merit rather than by grade</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Traps, reserves and jacket colours</h3><p>British greyhound races have a maximum of six runners, one from each trap. If a dog is withdrawn through injury or illness, a reserve can take its place when a suitable one is available. Reserves are marked with an ‘R’ on the racecard and on their racing jacket.</p><p>Each trap has its own jacket colour:</p><ul><li>Trap 1: Red</li><li>Trap 2: Blue</li><li>Trap 3: White</li><li>Trap 4: Black</li><li>Trap 5: Orange</li><li>Trap 6: Black and white stripes</li></ul><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="major-greyhound-races-our-tips-cover">Major Greyhound Races Our Tips Cover</h2><!--]--> <!----><h3>Greyhound Derby</h3><p>The English Greyhound Derby is the sport's flagship event, run over 500m with heats through May and the final in early June. Wimbledon hosted it from 1985 to 2016, before the track closed the following year. Since then it has been staged at Towcester, apart from 2019 and 2020 when Nottingham took over. Irish-trained runners have dominated recent renewals, as the table below shows. For heat-by-heat analysis, see our <a href="/blogs/greyhound-derby">Greyhound Derby preview</a>.</p><p>Greyhound Derby winners, last ten renewals (all over 500m):</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Venue</th><th scope="col">Winner (trap)</th><th scope="col">Trainer</th><th scope="col">SP</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Lennies Eddie (3)</td><td>Paul Hennessy (Ireland)</td><td>10/1</td><td>28.20</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Droopys Plunge (1)</td><td>Patrick Janssens (Towcester)</td><td>10/1</td><td>28.76</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Towcester</td><td>De Lahdedah (4)</td><td>Liam Dowling (Ireland)</td><td>5/1</td><td>28.58 (equalled track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Gaytime Nemo (2)</td><td>Graham Holland (Ireland)</td><td>9/1</td><td>28.89</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Romeo Magico (4)</td><td>Graham Holland (Ireland)</td><td>5/2</td><td>28.95</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Thorn Falcon (6)</td><td>Patrick Janssens (Towcester)</td><td>7/2</td><td>29.06</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Nottingham</td><td>Deerjet Sydney (2)</td><td>Pat Buckley (Ireland)</td><td>11/4</td><td>29.38</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>Nottingham</td><td>Priceless Blake (3)</td><td>Paul Hennessy (Ireland)</td><td>6/1</td><td>29.32</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Dorotas Wildcat (3)</td><td>Kevin Hutton (Towcester)</td><td>2/1</td><td>28.85</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>Towcester</td><td>Astute Missile (4)</td><td>Seamus Cahill (Hove)</td><td>28/1</td><td>28.92</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Greyhound Oaks</h3><p>The Oaks is the classic for bitches, run over 480m. It has had several homes in recent years and moved to Dunstall Park in 2025, where it is run in the autumn. Our <a href="/blogs/greyhound-oaks-betting-tips-stats-and-analysis">Greyhound Oaks tips</a> cover the contenders, stats and trends.</p><p>Greyhound Oaks winners, last ten renewals:</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Venue</th><th scope="col">Winner (trap)</th><th scope="col">Trainer</th><th scope="col">SP</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025</td><td>Dunstall Park (480m)</td><td>Butlers Lane (5)</td><td>Barry Denby (Nottingham)</td><td>7/2</td><td>27.95</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Perry Barr (480m)</td><td>Druids Say Go (2)</td><td>Patrick Janssens (Towcester)</td><td>4/11</td><td>27.82</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Perry Barr (480m)</td><td>No Rush (6)</td><td>Carol Weatherall (Monmore)</td><td>15/8</td><td>28.18</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Perry Barr (480m)</td><td>Slick Sakina (5)</td><td>Patrick Janssens (Towcester)</td><td>11/8</td><td>27.85 (track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>Perry Barr (480m)</td><td>Billys Diva (1)</td><td>Marie Kennedy (Ireland)</td><td>2/1</td><td>28.88</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Swindon (476m)</td><td>Ballymac Trend (3)</td><td>Angie Kibble (Swindon)</td><td>25/1</td><td>28.41</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>Swindon (476m)</td><td>Bull Run Byte (1)</td><td>Kevin Hutton (Monmore)</td><td>7/4</td><td>28.29</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Swindon (480m)</td><td>Ravenswood Flo (1)</td><td>Kevin Hutton (Monmore)</td><td>3/1</td><td>[time]</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>Belle Vue (470m)</td><td>Wuheida (2)</td><td>Phil Simmonds (Romford)</td><td>11/4</td><td>28.74</td></tr><tr><td>2016</td><td>Belle Vue (470m)</td><td>Forest Twilight (3)</td><td>Hayley Keightley (Private)</td><td>6/4</td><td>27.79</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Greyhound St Leger</h3><p>The St Leger is the premier staying classic. Like its horse racing namesake, it's a test for the stayers, and it's now run over 715m at Dunstall Park, with the final usually in September. Wembley, Wimbledon, Perry Barr and Nottingham have all hosted it in the past. Trainer Mark Wallis has an outstanding record in the race, with multiple wins since 2009. Read our <a href="/blogs/greyhound-st-leger-betting-guide-and-preview">Greyhound St Leger betting guide</a> for the full preview.</p><p>Greyhound St Leger winners, last ten renewals:</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Venue</th><th scope="col">Winner (trap)</th><th scope="col">Trainer</th><th scope="col">SP</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026</td><td>Dunstall Park (715m)</td><td>Droopys Flare (2)</td><td>Sean Parker (Sheffield)</td><td>1/4</td><td>43.17</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Nottingham (730m)</td><td>Mongys Wild (3)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Private)</td><td>5/6</td><td>44.55</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Droopys Clue (1)</td><td>Seamus Cahill (Hove)</td><td>2/5</td><td>42.97</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Droopys Clue (2)</td><td>Seamus Cahill (Hove)</td><td>4/9</td><td>42.33 (track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Havana Lover (5)</td><td>Liz McNair (Private)</td><td>11/10</td><td>43.10</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Space Jet (3)</td><td>Matt Dartnall (Central Park)</td><td>10/3</td><td>42.59 (track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Smallmead (2)</td><td>John Mullins (Yarmouth)</td><td>10/1</td><td>43.37</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Redzer Ardfert (1)</td><td>Brendan Maunsell (Ireland)</td><td>5/4</td><td>43.12</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Calico Ranger (6)</td><td>Carol Weatherall (Henlow)</td><td>9/4</td><td>43.09</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>Perry Barr (710m)</td><td>Rubys Rascal (1)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Towcester)</td><td>3/1</td><td>42.99</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>Essex Vase</h3><p>Run at Romford over 575m, the Essex Vase is one of the major six-bend competitions of the year. Its heats usually open the calendar in January, with the final in late January or early February. Ballyregan Bob, one of the sport's all-time greats, won it in 1985 and set a track record in the process, and Mark Wallis has won it a record six times.</p><p>Essex Vase winners, last ten renewals (all at Romford over 575m):</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Winner (trap)</th><th scope="col">Trainer</th><th scope="col">SP</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026</td><td>Tiffield Tarquin (6)</td><td>Roy Peckham (Yarmouth)</td><td>6/5</td><td>34.89</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Droopys Eunice (4)</td><td>Maxine Locke (Romford)</td><td>9/2</td><td>35.21</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Roxys Bullet (1)</td><td>Paul Burr (Yarmouth)</td><td>12/1</td><td>35.28</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Aayamza Magic (3)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Henlow)</td><td>10/3</td><td>35.32</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Coolavanny Aunty (2)</td><td>Angela Harrison (Newcastle)</td><td>10/11</td><td>35.25</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>Antigua Romeo (2)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Henlow)</td><td>8/1</td><td>34.66</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Kilmessan Puma (5)</td><td>David Mullins (Romford)</td><td>16/1</td><td>35.47</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>Droopys Aoife (1)</td><td>Ernest Gaskin Jr (Private)</td><td>9/2</td><td>35.08</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Prime Time (3)</td><td>Phil Simmonds (Romford)</td><td>7/1</td><td>35.34</td></tr><tr><td>2017*</td><td>Murrys Act (5)</td><td>Kevin Boon (Yarmouth)</td><td>2/1</td><td>35.34</td></tr></tbody></table><p>* The 2017 final was delayed and run in March 2018.</p><h3>TV Trophy</h3><p>The TV Trophy is the leading marathon event of the year. It moves between tracks and is run over the host track's marathon trip, with recent renewals staged at Oxford and Towcester. It takes its name from the BBC, which televised it from 1958 until 1996. The most celebrated winner is Scurlogue Champ, who won in 1985 and 1986. He was famous for starting slowly and then cutting through the field with a devastating finish, which made him a huge crowd favourite. Among current trainers, Mark Wallis and Paul Young have each won it five times.</p><p>TV Trophy winners, last ten renewals:</p><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Year</th><th scope="col">Venue</th><th scope="col">Winner (trap)</th><th scope="col">Trainer</th><th scope="col">SP</th><th scope="col">Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2026</td><td>Towcester (942m)</td><td>Droopys Rated (6)</td><td>Paul Young (Romford)</td><td>11/8</td><td>57.17 (equalled track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2025</td><td>Towcester (942m)</td><td>Mongys Wild (1)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Private)</td><td>[SP]</td><td>57.41</td></tr><tr><td>2024</td><td>Oxford (847m)</td><td>Bubbly Inferno (4)</td><td>Paul Young (Romford)</td><td>16/1</td><td>52.37 (track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2023</td><td>Crayford (874m)</td><td>Bellmore Sally (4)</td><td>James Fenwick (Newcastle)</td><td>10/11</td><td>56.38</td></tr><tr><td>2022</td><td>Hove (955m)</td><td>Space Jet (4)</td><td>Matt Dartnall (Central Park)</td><td>4/9</td><td>58.86</td></tr><tr><td>2021</td><td>Monmore (900m)</td><td>Aayamza Royale (4)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Henlow)</td><td>8/13</td><td>55.00 (track record)</td></tr><tr><td>2020</td><td>Hove (945m)</td><td>Aayamza Royale (4)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Henlow)</td><td>1/1</td><td>61.05</td></tr><tr><td>2019</td><td>Romford (925m)</td><td>Bumblebee Bullet (3)</td><td>Mark Wallis (Henlow)</td><td>14/1</td><td>59.26</td></tr><tr><td>2018</td><td>Crayford (874m)</td><td>Savana Winner (4)</td><td>Diane Henry (Henlow)</td><td>4/1</td><td>56.87</td></tr><tr><td>2017</td><td>Towcester (906m)</td><td>Goldies Hotspur (5)</td><td>Patrick Janssens (Towcester)</td><td>16/1</td><td>55.45</td></tr></tbody></table><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="what-trap-number-wins-most-often-in-greyhound-racing">What trap number wins most often in greyhound racing?</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>With six runners, each trap would win around 16.7% of the time if the draw made no difference. In practice, the figures vary from track to track depending on the shape of the circuit and the run to the first bend. A trap that wins well above that baseline at a particular track may point to a draw bias worth factoring into your selections.</p><p>The chart below shows the win percentage for each trap at every GBGB track we cover in 2025. This is the share of runners from that trap that went on to win. The number beside each track name is the number of races included.</p><iframe scrolling="no" loading="lazy" frameborder="0" title="Interactive or visual content" sandbox="allow-same-origin allow-forms allow-scripts allow-downloads allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox allow-top-navigation-by-user-activation" style="width: 100%; height: 360px;" class="flourish-embed-iframe" src="https://flo.uri.sh/visualisation/21001210/embed?auto=1"></iframe><h3>Best-performing trap at each track</h3><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Track</th><th scope="col">Races</th><th scope="col">Best trap</th><th scope="col">Win %</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Central Park</td><td>2,911</td><td>Trap 6</td><td>19.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Doncaster</td><td>2,759</td><td>Trap 6</td><td>22.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Dunstall Park</td><td>725</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>22.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Harlow</td><td>3,762</td><td>Trap 6</td><td>23.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Hove</td><td>2,993</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>22.7%</td></tr><tr><td>Kinsley</td><td>2,427</td><td>Trap 6</td><td>18.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Monmore</td><td>3,672</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>19.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Newcastle</td><td>2,940</td><td>Trap 3</td><td>19.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Nottingham</td><td>2,407</td><td>Trap 2</td><td>18.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Pelaw Grange</td><td>1,139</td><td>Trap 3</td><td>20.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Romford</td><td>3,655</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>19.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Sheffield</td><td>2,901</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>21.7%</td></tr><tr><td>Sunderland</td><td>2,852</td><td>Trap 4</td><td>18.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Towcester</td><td>3,392</td><td>Trap 1</td><td>23.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Valley</td><td>1,985</td><td>Trap 6</td><td>21.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Yarmouth</td><td>2,239</td><td>Trap 3</td><td>20.6%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The strongest biases in 2025 were towards the wide draw at Harlow, where Trap 6 won 23.3% of the time, and the inside draw at Towcester, where Trap 1 won 23.0%. Trap 1 also did well at Hove, Dunstall Park and Sheffield, while Trap 6 stood out at Doncaster and Valley. Small samples, such as Dunstall Park in its first months, should be treated with more caution.</p><p>Before placing a bet on the dogs, compare the options on our <a href="/bookmakers/articles/best-greyhounds">best greyhound betting sites</a> page.</p><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="how-often-does-the-favourite-win-at-each-greyhound-track">How often does the favourite win at each greyhound track?</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>Knowing how often favourites win at each track helps you weigh up a tip on the market leader. In graded racing, the favourite typically wins about a third of the time, but that varies noticeably from track to track. It's also higher in open races, where the gap in class between runners tends to be wider.</p><h3>Favourites in graded races (2025)</h3><div class="table-responsive"><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Track</th><th scope="col">Outright favourites</th><th scope="col">Wins</th><th scope="col">Win %</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Valley</td><td>1,750</td><td>743</td><td>42.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Dunstall Park</td><td>587</td><td>234</td><td>39.9%</td></tr><tr><td>Towcester</td><td>2,753</td><td>1,079</td><td>39.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Doncaster</td><td>2,351</td><td>908</td><td>38.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Pelaw Grange</td><td>697</td><td>259</td><td>37.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Sheffield</td><td>2,533</td><td>926</td><td>36.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Yarmouth</td><td>1,985</td><td>714</td><td>36.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Central Park</td><td>2,439</td><td>874</td><td>35.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Hove</td><td>2,357</td><td>843</td><td>35.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Monmore</td><td>3,203</td><td>1,109</td><td>34.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Romford</td><td>2,925</td><td>1,006</td><td>34.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Harlow</td><td>3,587</td><td>1,227</td><td>34.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Newcastle</td><td>2,613</td><td>892</td><td>34.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Nottingham</td><td>1,958</td><td>668</td><td>34.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Sunderland</td><td>2,456</td><td>818</td><td>33.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Kinsley</td><td>2,224</td><td>691</td><td>31.1%</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">All tracks</th><td>38,907</td><td>13,915</td><td>35.8%</td></tr></tfoot></table></div><p>Valley had the best strike rate for graded favourites in 2025, while Kinsley again finished bottom at 31.1%. If a Kinsley tip is on the favourite, it's worth a closer look before backing it.</p><h3>Favourites in open races (2025)</h3><div class="table-responsive"><table class="table table-bordered table-striped text-left no-branding" style="width: 100%;"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Track</th><th scope="col">Outright favourites</th><th scope="col">Wins</th><th scope="col">Win %</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nottingham</td><td>286</td><td>151</td><td>52.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Dunstall Park</td><td>100</td><td>52</td><td>52.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Sunderland</td><td>245</td><td>127</td><td>51.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Towcester</td><td>428</td><td>221</td><td>51.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Monmore</td><td>246</td><td>120</td><td>48.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Doncaster</td><td>219</td><td>106</td><td>48.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Newcastle</td><td>193</td><td>93</td><td>48.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Central Park</td><td>353</td><td>166</td><td>47.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Pelaw Grange</td><td>379</td><td>178</td><td>47.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Hove</td><td>491</td><td>228</td><td>46.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Yarmouth</td><td>155</td><td>71</td><td>45.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Valley</td><td>112</td><td>51</td><td>45.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Sheffield</td><td>214</td><td>90</td><td>42.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Romford</td><td>545</td><td>228</td><td>41.8%</td></tr></tbody><tfoot><tr><th scope="row">All tracks</th><td>4,358</td><td>2,094</td><td>48.0%</td></tr></tfoot></table></div><p>Harlow and Kinsley held no open races in 2025.</p><p>Towcester, which has hosted the Greyhound Derby in recent years, regularly ranks among the top five tracks for open-race favourites. Its wide, galloping circuit tends to keep crowding and baulking to a minimum, so the best dog on the day has a better chance of getting a clear run.</p><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="map-of-uk-greyhound-tracks">Map of UK Greyhound Tracks</h2><!--]--> <!----><p><picture data-copyright style="width: 300px;" class="fr-fic fr-dib shrink-0 max-w-full"><source media="(min-width: 768px)" srcset="https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/charts-and-graphics/uks-greyhound-tracks-map-aug2026-2.png"><source media="(min-width: 480px)" srcset="https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/charts-and-graphics/uks-greyhound-tracks-map-aug2026-2.png"><img src="https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/charts-and-graphics/uks-greyhound-tracks-map-aug2026-2.png" loading="lazy" class="w-full"></picture>The map below shows where each of the greyhound tracks our tips cover is located, from Newcastle and Sunderland in the north-east down to Hove on the south coast.</p><p><br></p><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[0--><h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl" id="who-writes-our-greyhound-racing-content">Who Writes Our Greyhound Racing Content?</h2><!--]--> <!----><p>This page is written by Darren Brett, who has more than 30 years' experience in racing and betting, including roles at The Jockey Club and SIS LIVE. He has been betting on greyhound racing for decades and, as manager of OLBG's Tipster Competition, oversees the tips posted across the site.</p><div class="author-box-component mb-8 p-6 bg-[#414a5e] text-white rounded-md items-center md:items-start justify-center md:justify-start flex flex-col md:flex-row color-dark  compact"><a class="rounded-full flex justify-center items-center overflow-hidden w-24 h-24 mb-6 md:mr-6 border-[6px] border-gray-500 border-opacity-25 image-outside" href="/meet-the-experts/darren-brett"><picture><source srcset="https://olbgimages.co.uk/image-cache?src=https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/october-app/uploads/public/649/ec7/564/649ec7564fbfa346637961.jpeg&amp;w=80&amp;h=80&amp;zc=1" media="(max-width: 1023px)"> <source srcset="https://olbgimages.co.uk/image-cache?src=https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/october-app/uploads/public/649/ec7/564/649ec7564fbfa346637961.jpeg&amp;w=100&amp;h=100&amp;zc=1" media="(min-width: 1024px)"> <img alt="Darren Brett" src="https://olbgimages.co.uk/image-cache?src=https://olbgimages.co.uk/storage/gallery/october-app/uploads/public/649/ec7/564/649ec7564fbfa346637961.jpeg&amp;w=80&amp;h=80&amp;zc=1" title="Darren Brett" class="w-full h-auto" width="80" height="80" loading="lazy"></picture></a> <div class="flex flex-col flex-1 items-center text-center md:items-start md:text-left"><p class="text-xl font-bold">About the author</p>  <div class="mb-2">Darren Brett has worked in racing and betting for more than 30 years, with a background at The Jockey Club and SIS LIVE. He manages OLBG's Tipster Competition, overseeing thousands of tips every week, and has bet on greyhound racing for decades. He writes OLBG's greyhound betting guides, covering how to read the form, the main bet types and where to bet.</div> <p class="mb-0 author-box-info">- <a class="font-bold underline text-olbg-blue-gray hover:text-olbg-orange" href="/meet-the-experts/darren-brett">Darren Brett</a>, Content Editor</p></div></div><!----></div></div></section><section class="py-2 border-t lg:py-8 first:pt-0 first:mt-8 sct"><div class="container relative z-10 "><div class="content"><!--[-1--><!--]--> <!----><div><header class="sct-hdr  "><h3 class="text-2xl"><i class="text-2xl i-ui-faq text-olbg-orange"></i> FAQ</h3> <p class="mb-2 text-gray-400 tagline">Greyhound Racing Tips FAQs</p></header> <div class="faq"><div class="flex flex-col gap-0 sm:gap-3 sm:flex-row fq-cnt"><ul class="flex-1 [&amp;&amp;]:p-0 [&amp;&amp;]:m-0 h-rst-ls"><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="1"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">Are OLBG's greyhound racing tips free?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>Yes. Every greyhound tip on this page is free. The tips are posted by OLBG members, and each race shows the most-tipped dog along with how many tips it has received and the comments behind the picks.</p></div></li><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="2"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">How do you pick a winner in a greyhound race?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>Start with the tips on this page, which show the most-tipped dog in each race along with the reasoning behind the picks, and compare them with your own view of the form.</p><p>Early pace is one of the most important factors. Races are often decided by the run to the first bend, where crowding and bumping are common, so a dog that is quick out of the traps and likely to lead avoids most of the trouble. Check each dog's recent sectional (first-bend) times as well as its overall run times.</p><p>Also consider whether a dog's running style suits its trap. A dog that likes to run close to the rail is better drawn inside than out wide. Look for dogs moving down in grade, or young, improving dogs that could still be ahead of their mark.</p></div></li><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="3"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">What trap number wins the most in greyhound racing?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>No single trap wins most often at every track. In 2025, Trap 1 was the best-performing trap at six tracks and Trap 6 at five, while Traps 2, 3 and 4 each came out on top elsewhere.</p><p>With six runners, each trap would win around 16.7% of the time if the draw made no difference. The biggest biases in 2025 were Trap 6 at Harlow and Trap 1 at Towcester, both winning more than 23% of the time. Bias depends mainly on each track's layout and the run to the first bend, so check the track-by-track trap figures on this page before betting.</p></div></li><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="4"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">Does the favourite have a good record in greyhound racing?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>Yes. In 2025, the outright favourite won 35.8% of graded races across the tracks in our table, roughly one race in three. That varied from 31.1% at Kinsley to 42.5% at Valley.</p><p>Favourites win more often in open races, where the gap in class between runners tends to be wider. In 2025, open-race favourites won 48.0% of the time overall, from 54.1% at Oxford down to 41.8% at Romford. You'll find the full track-by-track figures further up this page.</p></div></li></ul><ul class="flex-1 [&amp;&amp;]:p-0 [&amp;&amp;]:m-0 h-rst-ls"><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="1"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">Can I watch greyhound racing on TV?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>Yes. Sky Sports Racing shows selected greyhound meetings, mainly evening open racing and the big events. Almost every race at GBGB tracks is also streamed live through bookmaker websites and apps, where you'll usually need a funded account or a qualifying bet to watch.</p></div></li><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="2"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">What type of greyhound bets do OLBG tips cover?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>OLBG's greyhound tips are win tips, so every selection on this page is a pick to win the race. The win market is the simplest greyhound bet: if your dog finishes first, your bet wins.</p><p>Bookmakers also offer bets such as forecasts (first and second in the correct order) and tricasts (first, second and third in the correct order). Our <a href="/bookmakers/articles/greyhounds-betting-advice">greyhound betting guide</a> explains these and other bet types in more detail.</p></div></li><li data-faq-card="" class="group p-2 px-3 [&amp;&amp;]:mb-3 hover:bg-[#fff6e8] hover:cursor-pointer list-none bg-white border rounded border-c-border-2 shadow-sm faq-crd" data-num="3"><h4 class="p-0 m-0"><button type="button" data-faq-toggle="" aria-expanded="false" class="flex items-center w-full p-0 m-0 text text-left bg-transparent border-0 qa-title"><span class="flex-1 max-md:!text-md max-md:leading-relaxed max-md:pr-10 md:!text-base">How do handicaps work in greyhound racing?</span> <i class="i-ui-dropdown-arrow text-olbg-orange group-[.card-opened]:-rotate-90 text-xs"></i></button></h4> <div class="hidden group-[.card-opened]:block pb-0 mt-2 mb-0 answer"><p>In a handicap race, dogs of differing ability start from staggered positions to even out their chances. The fastest dog starts furthest back, known as running off "scratch", while slower dogs are given a head start. Each dog's starting allowance is shown on the racecard. Handicaps are much less common than graded races at British tracks.</p></div></li></ul></div></div></div> <div><script type="application/ld+json">
